  "question": "Background / Context\n\nThe project involves developing a database system for the Farmer Welfare Department to facilitate the rental of agricultural machines and equipment by farmers. The system is aligned with the administrative structure of rural governance, particularly the grampanchayat, which oversees multiple villages and is responsible for their development. Each village is divided into wards, each having a representative member who supervises the rental operations.\n\n Objectives\n\n- Develop a structured database to manage machine rentals for farmers.\n- Enable efficient tracking of equipment availability, rentals, and payments.\n- Support the administrative needs of the grampanchayat in overseeing village and ward-level operations.\n\n Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Farmers: Primary users who rent machinery for agricultural purposes.\n- Ward Members: Representatives responsible for supervising machine rentals.\n- Grampanchayat Officials: Administrators overseeing village development and operations.\n\n Functional Descriptions\n\n1. Grampanchayat Management:\n   - Maintain records of grampanchayats with details such as total wards, taluka name, and total villages.\n\n2. Village and Ward Management:\n   - Organize data for villages and wards, including identifying details and hierarchical structure.\n\n3. Farmer Registration:\n   - Store farmer information, including unique identification through AADH_NO, name, and gender.\n\n4. Machine Rental:\n   - Enable farmers to rent machines identified by MACH_ID.\n   - Capture rental details like duration and associated ward and member details.\n\n5. Billing and Payments:\n   - Generate and manage billing information, capturing amounts, payment methods, and dates.\n   - Ensure bills are uniquely identified by BILL_ID.\n\n Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Scalability: The system should support a large number of records for wards, villages, farmers, and machines.\n- Security: Ensure sensitive data, such as farmer identification, is securely managed.\n- Usability: Provide a user-friendly interface for ward members and grampanchayat officials to manage operations efficiently.\n- Reliability: Maintain accurate and consistent data for all transactions and records.",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0218\nCREATE TABLE `grampanchayat` (\n  `grampanchayat_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `taluka_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `total_wards` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `total_villages` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`grampanchayat_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `village` (\n  `village_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `grampanchayat_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `village_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`village_id`),\n  KEY `grampanchayat_id` (`grampanchayat_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `village_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`grampanchayat_id`) REFERENCES `grampanchayat` (`grampanchayat_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `ward` (\n  `ward_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `village_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `ward_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `member_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `member_phone` varchar(20) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`ward_id`),\n  KEY `village_id` (`village_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `ward_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`village_id`) REFERENCES `village` (`village_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `farmer` (\n  `aad_h_no` char(12) NOT NULL,\n  `farmer_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `gender` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `village_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `ward_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`aad_h_no`),\n  KEY `village_id` (`village_id`),\n  KEY `ward_id` (`ward_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `farmer_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`village_id`) REFERENCES `village` (`village_id`) ON DELETE SET NULL ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `farmer_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`ward_id`) REFERENCES `ward` (`ward_id`) ON DELETE SET NULL ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `machine` (\n  `mach_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `machine_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `availability_status` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`mach_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `rental` (\n  `rental_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `aad_h_no` char(12) NOT NULL,\n  `mach_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `ward_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `member_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `rental_start_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `rental_end_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `duration_days` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`rental_id`),\n  KEY `aad_h_no` (`aad_h_no`),\n  KEY `mach_id` (`mach_id`),\n  KEY `ward_id` (`ward_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `rental_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`aad_h_no`) REFERENCES `farmer` (`aad_h_no`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `rental_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`mach_id`) REFERENCES `machine` (`mach_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `rental_ibfk_3` FOREIGN KEY (`ward_id`) REFERENCES `ward` (`ward_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `bill` (\n  `bill_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `rental_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `amount` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `payment_method` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `payment_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`bill_id`),\n  KEY `rental_id` (`rental_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `bill_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`rental_id`) REFERENCES `rental` (`rental_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
